<Docs title="Trust and pairing" slug="trust" lede="Your systems trust each other directly. You decide for how long." description="How Daisy pairing works, how long trust lasts, and how to forget a peer.">
  <h2>Pairing</h2>
  <p>Each new system pairs once. It shows a six-digit code and you type it on the system it is joining. That code is used to prove, cryptographically, that both systems are the ones you meant. From then on, they recognize each other without a code, and a system that joins a group is introduced to every member.</p>
  <p>A system accepts a new one only while it is sharing and:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>it has no peers yet,</li>
    <li>someone chose <strong>Add a System</strong> on it in the last 30 seconds (this closes after one success), or</li>
    <li><strong>Always discoverable</strong> is on under <strong>Advanced…</strong>. Turning it on asks for Touch ID or the login password.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A system that does not accept new systems refuses an unknown one without showing a code. Each accepts at most five attempts in ten minutes.</p>

  <h2>How long trust lasts</h2>
  <p>Each system trusts a new peer until it goes unused for four days, and keeps its own choice; change it from the peer's row. Changing it later starts it over from that moment. Both must still trust each other to connect, so the stricter choice wins.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Choice</th><th>Trusted until</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Until unused for a time you choose (4 days by default)</td><td>That many hours or days pass without a connection. Staying connected renews it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>This session</td><td>The session ends. After an accidental drop, not a deliberate disconnect, the two systems may reconnect within 60 seconds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Until I forget</td><td>You forget it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>When trust ends, the two systems pair again with a new code.</p>

  <h2>Forget a peer</h2>
  <p>Forget one peer, several, or all of them. A session with a forgotten peer ends within a second.</p>

  <h2>Start over with a new key</h2>
  <p>Each system has its own key. Replacing it means every peer paired with it must pair again. Use this if you think a system's key has been copied.</p>

  <p>The <a href={href("/docs/security-model/")}>security model</a> explains the cryptography, and <a href={href("/docs/user-guide/")}>User guide</a> covers the app and the optional command line.</p>
</Docs>
